1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,537
Aarav opens an old radio.

2
00:00:02,537 --> 00:00:05,098
Inside are dozens of tiny parts.

3
00:00:05,098 --> 00:00:10,512
Striped beads, small orange discs,
blue cans and coils of copper wire.

4
00:00:10,862 --> 00:00:14,177
On the circuit diagram, they
are just lines and zigzags.

5
00:00:14,177 --> 00:00:17,608
So how do you match the drawing
to the part in your hand?

6
00:00:18,108 --> 00:00:19,548
You need two skills.

7
00:00:19,548 --> 00:00:22,766
Reading the symbol, and
recognising the real part.

8
00:00:23,116 --> 00:00:24,768
Let's start with symbols.

9
00:00:24,768 --> 00:00:27,232
Each passive part has a basic symbol.

10
00:00:27,582 --> 00:00:29,318
A resistor is a zigzag.

11
00:00:29,318 --> 00:00:32,474
A capacitor is two
parallel plates with a gap.

12
00:00:32,474 --> 00:00:34,741
An inductor is a coil of loops.

13
00:00:35,091 --> 00:00:37,827
Now add small marks to change the meaning.

14
00:00:37,827 --> 00:00:40,852
An arrow drawn across the
symbol means variable.

15
00:00:40,852 --> 00:00:43,491
You can change its value while it works.

16
00:00:43,841 --> 00:00:47,136
An arrow pointing at the
resistor is a potentiometer.

17
00:00:47,136 --> 00:00:51,570
It has three terminals, and the arrow
is the sliding contact, called the

18
00:00:51,570 --> 00:00:52,470
wiper.

19
00:00:52,505 --> 00:00:55,251
A short bar instead of
an arrow means a preset.

20
00:00:55,251 --> 00:00:58,170
You set it once with a
screwdriver, and leave it.

21
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:02,583
For capacitors, a plus sign and
one curved plate mean polarised.

22
00:01:02,583 --> 00:01:07,255
That is an electrolytic capacitor,
and it must go in the right way round.

23
00:01:07,605 --> 00:01:11,674
For inductors, straight lines
beside the coil mean an iron core.

24
00:01:11,674 --> 00:01:13,858
Dashed lines mean a ferrite core.

25
00:01:13,858 --> 00:01:16,690
Two coils sharing a
core make a transformer.

26
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:20,828
Let's see the potentiometer
at work on the breadboard.

27
00:01:20,828 --> 00:01:22,553
It is a volume control.

28
00:01:22,903 --> 00:01:25,940
A music signal goes across
the whole resistor track.

29
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:30,062
The wiper picks off just a part of
it, and sends it to the speaker.

30
00:01:30,412 --> 00:01:31,655
Watch the scope.

31
00:01:31,655 --> 00:01:33,635
The top trace is the input.

32
00:01:33,635 --> 00:01:34,929
It never changes.

33
00:01:35,279 --> 00:01:38,465
Slide the wiper down, and
the output wave shrinks.

34
00:01:38,465 --> 00:01:40,006
The music gets quieter.

35
00:01:40,356 --> 00:01:43,321
Slide it back up, and
the output grows again.

36
00:01:43,321 --> 00:01:46,768
The arrow in the symbol is
exactly this sliding contact.

37
00:01:47,418 --> 00:01:49,729
The symbol tells you what a part does.

38
00:01:49,729 --> 00:01:51,670
The markings tell you its value.

39
00:01:52,470 --> 00:01:53,886
Now, the real parts.

40
00:01:53,886 --> 00:01:55,534
How do you recognise them?

41
00:01:55,884 --> 00:02:00,439
A carbon or metal film resistor is
a small bead with coloured bands.

42
00:02:00,439 --> 00:02:02,290
The bands give its value.

43
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:06,985
A ceramic disc capacitor is a flat
disc, often with a three digit code

44
00:02:06,985 --> 00:02:08,079
printed on it.

45
00:02:08,429 --> 00:02:11,116
An electrolytic capacitor is a small can.

46
00:02:11,116 --> 00:02:15,957
A stripe down one side marks the minus
lead, and the longer lead is plus.

47
00:02:16,307 --> 00:02:20,725
An inductor often looks like a coil
of copper wire, sometimes wound on

48
00:02:20,725 --> 00:02:23,098
a ring or a small rod of ferrite.

49
00:02:23,748 --> 00:02:27,180
So how do you read that three
digit code on a capacitor?

50
00:02:27,530 --> 00:02:30,472
The first two digits are
the significant figures.

51
00:02:31,172 --> 00:02:33,375
The third digit is the multiplier.

52
00:02:33,375 --> 00:02:35,740
It tells you how many zeros to add.

53
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:39,332
And the answer is always in picofarads.

54
00:02:39,332 --> 00:02:42,853
One picofarad is ten to
the minus twelve farads.

55
00:02:43,553 --> 00:02:46,926
One thousand picofarads
make one nanofarad.

56
00:02:46,926 --> 00:02:50,360
One thousand nanofarads
make one microfarad.

57
00:02:51,010 --> 00:02:52,232
Worked example.

58
00:02:52,232 --> 00:02:55,265
A disc capacitor is marked one zero four.

59
00:02:55,615 --> 00:02:57,851
Ten, followed by four zeros.

60
00:02:57,851 --> 00:03:00,699
That is one hundred thousand picofarads.

61
00:03:01,299 --> 00:03:03,061
Divide by a thousand.

62
00:03:03,061 --> 00:03:04,869
One hundred nanofarads.

63
00:03:05,469 --> 00:03:07,738
Divide by a thousand again.

64
00:03:07,738 --> 00:03:09,936
Zero point one microfarads.

65
00:03:09,936 --> 00:03:14,725
So one zero four is the famous
point one microfarad capacitor.

66
00:03:15,375 --> 00:03:16,840
Now, the exam trap.

67
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:21,763
Many students think an electrolytic
capacitor can be connected either way

68
00:03:21,763 --> 00:03:22,663
round.

69
00:03:22,403 --> 00:03:23,315
It cannot.

70
00:03:23,665 --> 00:03:26,538
Its plus lead must go
to the higher voltage.

71
00:03:26,538 --> 00:03:30,429
Connected backwards, it can
heat up, bulge, or even burst.

72
00:03:31,079 --> 00:03:32,654
Where is this asked?

73
00:03:32,654 --> 00:03:34,228
In PUC board papers.

74
00:03:34,228 --> 00:03:39,217
Draw the symbols of components, identify
a component, or decode a capacitor

75
00:03:39,217 --> 00:03:40,117
marking.

76
00:03:40,617 --> 00:03:41,517
Try this.

77
00:03:41,472 --> 00:03:44,526
A ceramic capacitor is
marked two two three.

78
00:03:44,526 --> 00:03:46,288
What is its capacitance?

79
00:03:51,688 --> 00:03:52,588
C.

80
00:03:52,234 --> 00:03:55,442
Zero point zero two two microfarads.

81
00:03:55,792 --> 00:03:58,124
The first two digits are twenty two.

82
00:03:58,124 --> 00:04:01,173
The third digit, three,
means add three zeros.

83
00:04:01,523 --> 00:04:06,168
That is twenty two thousand picofarads,
which is twenty two nanofarads.

84
00:04:06,518 --> 00:04:12,199
Divide by a thousand once more, and you
get zero point zero two two microfarads.

85
00:04:12,849 --> 00:04:14,370
Screenshot this card.

86
00:04:17,570 --> 00:04:20,796
Next, reading component
specifications and datasheets.

87
00:04:20,796 --> 00:04:24,012
What else does a part tell
you, beyond its value?
